<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hersir">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hersir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hersir was a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> military commander of a hundred, a county subdivision of about 100 men, who owed allegiance to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> or king. The term first meant a wealthy farmer who owned land and had the status of a leader, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> came to mean one who organised and led raids. From the 10th century the hersirs' influence declined as effective national monarchies developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and their independence as military leaders gave way to the title of a royal representative. Known hersirs include the Norwegian Ketill Flatnose of the 9th century and the Swede Jarlabanke Ingefastsson, who raised about 20 runestones with his relatives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Hersirs were also aspiring landowners who, like the middle class in many feudal societies, supported the kings as they centralised power. The Hávamál, the mythical advice of the god Odin to humankind, praises careful thought and well-planned attack, and in its oral form it was one of the main influences on the outlook of the hersir. Their religion seems to have lain somewhere between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and paganism: some hersirs were baptised, most often to improve trade with Christians or in obedience to a new king rather than for religious reasons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The hersir's main weapon was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> sword with a Type H hilt, the most common hilt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, made according to the owner's wealth of polished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> with inlays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>; its blade, of Geibig Type 3, dates from the second half of the 8th to the second half of the 10th century. A wooden shield went with it. Armour displayed wealth as much as it protected, and a hersir often wore a rounded or conical helmet and a short mail coat, of which the finds at Gjermundbu are an example.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> travelled long distances on horseback and overseas in longboats that carried thirty people, but they always fought on foot. In the shield wall the hersir stood at the front, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and slingers behind, the men standing close so that their shields overlapped; the svinfylking varied this with several wedges pointing at the enemy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigs-ula">Rígsþula</a>, Hersir is the name of a man whose daughter Erna married Jarl, the first earl. The Sagas of Icelanders mention several hersirs, among them Bjorn Buna Grimsson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sognefjord">Sognefjord</a>, who lived at Aurland and was the father of Ketill Flatnose; Ketill's daughter was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aud-the-deep-minded-ketilsdottir">Aud the Deep-Minded</a>. Landnáma traces Bjorn's descent through his foremothers to Thorgerdur Eylaugsdottir, a female hersir of Sogn, which is rare in these stories and points to her importance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hersir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Norway a <b>hersir</b> commanded the men of his district and owed loyalty to a jarl or king; the later sagas give each jarl four or more hersar.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
